WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.689
Film Courage: What mistakes do writers make
writing characters?

00:00:02.689 --> 00:00:11.900
Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: I think the
challenge with characters that I have is creating

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:19.320
three dimensional people that feel real and
interesting to the audience, compelling and

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.289
intriguing and sympathetic are the things
that you want them to be.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:26.890
It’s not so much that you are writing the
character because you are writing the script

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.900
that has the character in it.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.020
Prior to writing the script you are figuring
out who the character is and what you are

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.610
going to present about that character to the
audience.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:43.120
I think it’s kind of harder than it looks
to come up with a character that feels real,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.760
feels like they are recalling coming from
real life some how and is really interesting

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.710
and that audience will care enough about them
and want to follow them because they are going

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.550
to relate to them on some level.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:01.730
It’s like figuring out what are the key
aspects of the character that defined them

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.260
for this movie or this TV series.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.180
What are the problems that they are mainly
dealing with?

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.110
What are their internal issues (their main
internal issues) and their main external problem

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:17.390
and then trying to write in such a way that
they are not just a tool of the story or the

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:24.422
concept but that they actually feel like the
audience gets drawn into the reality of their

00:01:24.422 --> 00:01:25.422
life.

00:01:25.422 --> 00:01:28.780
It feels super real (some how) the way that
the character is drawn.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:34.570
It’s not easy to do that but the best characters
feel like this is a real flesh and blood human

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:41.080
being and it has to do with they are not just
a stereotype, they have elements.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:45.930
They might start with one aspect that could
be a cliche or a stereotype or a trope or

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.159
a one-dimensional feature.

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:53.000
But then they have something else going on
that is surprising and very different.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.290
I read an article online (I think in Script
Reader Pro website) where they talked about

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.340
lengthy character bios aren’t really that
helpful.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.430
It’s better to thinking of your characters
as one main quality that most people would

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:13.640
define them as and then one surprising kind
of twist that you wouldn’t expect.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:19.150
So they use the example of AMERICAN BEAUTY
and they talk about Wes Bentley’s character

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:25.600
is a wise beyond his years character, so that’s
his main quality and he’s also a dealer

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.440
which is like a weird thing that you wouldn’t
expect.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.120
And each of the main characters in the movie
they defined in that way.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.560
Mena Suvari’s character is like this flirty
lady but in reality she is the insecure virgin.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.040
So there are like these surprising things
that things quality with this opposite or

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.810
different quality.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:52.620
But finding this first quality even is important
because it’s easy to kind of be vague about

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.040
who these people are.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:59.511
You want to think in terms of if a stranger
(which is what your audience is) were to look

00:02:59.511 --> 00:03:03.130
at this character in their life what would
be the one word description they would have

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.470
of the type of person that is?

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:08.680
Not what they look like, the type of person
that they are.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.890
What’s the main quality that comes through
and sort of figuring out what that quality

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:19.370
is and finding a way to subvert it to make
them more dimensional seems like a good thing

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:20.950
something I’ve been playing with lately.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:26.420
Film Courage: And even in GOODWILL HUNTING
that the Matt Damon character is a janitor

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:32.640
that can solve this unbelievable math equation
seems like “this will never work.”

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.510
But then when you watch him set against all
of these other characters, he seems like a

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.290
real guy…like this person exists to me?

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.330
Do you see what I’m saying?

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:46.520
Erik: But he is also an example of these two
opposing qualities, one quality being that

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:53.370
he’s sort of this working class tough guy
janitor street kid who has got a chip on his

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:58.920
shoulder who happens to be a brilliant genius,
like two things you wouldn’t think go together.

